{"step_index":5968,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T00:48:47Z","thinking":"Initial styling for the server management screen will focus on a grid layout for organization. This grid will have two columns with defined gaps and bottom margin. Server card styling includes background and border properties.\n\n\nServer card hover effect will increase the shadow and slightly lift the card. An active provider will have a primary border and distinct shadow. The header includes flexbox properties for alignment and spacing of the title and icon.\n\nStatus badges use pill styling and color-code online/offline states with corresponding backgrounds. The grid layout dictates card arrangement with gaps and margins for visual clarity.\n\n\nServer details will be presented in a styled list, providing clear labels and values for each attribute. Action buttons are organized using a grid layout.  An active server will be highlighted with a prominent banner, displaying key information.\n\n\nAnalyzing the `showScreen` function, it appears to manage screen visibility and update the top bar heading based on the active screen ID. The function either calls an original implementation or directly modifies the DOM to show/hide screens and updates the header text using a translation object.\n\n\nThe `showScreen` function is designed to handle screen transitions by removing the 'active' class from all buttons and then adding it to the selected button. It updates the top bar heading and calls specific functions for different screen IDs, such as `resetNewProcessForm()`, `loadLogs()`, and `loadServersUI()`.\n\nThe `loadServersUI()` function retrieves server information, iterates through the server list, updates the status badge based on running status, and adjusts button labels and actions accordingly. It determines the \"active server\" and styles the corresponding server card, potentially disabling or highlighting a \"set active\" button.\n\n\nThe code determines the active server and updates the UI accordingly, changing button states and highlighting the active server card, including banner updates. The server management function initiates or terminates a server instance based on user input, displaying status messages and updating the UI on success or failure.  A server testing function pings a specified server, updates a test output panel with results, including latency, gateway, and default model information.\n\n\nThe server testing function updates the UI with success or failure messages based on the API response, displaying latency for successful tests. Setting an active server triggers a call to update server status and also updates other UI elements upon success. Opening a server session involves another API call to initiate authentication, displaying a success or error message. The server status update interval can refresh the server UI when the server screen is active.\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nThe next step is to insert the navigation menu button and the new screen HTML content in the correct code locations. Translations for Arabic and English will be integrated into the relevant language files. Top bar titles will be updated using the provided translation keys. Finally, I will implement the Javascript functions within the designated lines to complete the feature. Careful verification of translation keys is required to ensure accurate localization.\n\n\nTranslation keys are added for the newly introduced UI elements, including labels, buttons, and status messages, ensuring both English and Arabic language support.  I've reviewed the existing translation file to insert the new keys and their corresponding English translations. The Arabic translations are being cross-verified for accuracy.\n\n\nThe HTML for the server management screen confirms the screen's structure and intended functionality, including a header, description, and an \"active provider\" banner. The banner suggests dynamic content indicating the currently active server. The translation keys confirm localization support.\n\n\nInitial UI elements are now designed, including a refresh button and a server card grid. Server cards display status badges and an \"active\" badge for the currently selected server. Details such as server port are included within the card content, alongside server icons and title information.\n\n\nThe code extracts server details, including port, model, service URL, and directory path, and presents them within a structured layout. A grid displays action buttons. The details reveal the server is listening on port 8001, running the 'gemini-3.8-flash' model, and the service URL points to a local endpoint, and the installation directory is identified.\n\n\nInitial server card structure includes buttons to manage server instances, test connections, and open sessions.  Each card presents the server title, model and port information, using translated labels.\n\n\nThe UI now reflects server status and details, including port, model, and service URL. The UI displays an \"active server\" badge, reflecting running status. Server details are dynamically populated in the UI elements.\n\n\nThe server card HTML is now built, and its elements are populated with server-specific information, including directory and action buttons. The action buttons are wired to functions for toggling the server instance, testing the connection, and opening a session. Further, the UI elements are correctly reflecting details such as the server directory path.\n\n\nThe new diagnostic log panel is being added to the screen, including a header, a clear button and a dedicated output area. The current design will need a corresponding translation key for the title. Verification is needed to ensure that the server status indicator in the sidebar footer functions as expected.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"d:/AI_Engineer/Pharmacy-agy/desktop_app/templates/index.